The Nautilus DevOps team is expanding their AWS infrastructure and requires the setup of a private Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) along with a subnet. 
This VPC and subnet configuration will ensure that resources deployed within them remain isolated from external networks and can only communicate within the VPC. 
Additionally, the team needs to provision an EC2 instance under the newly created private VPC. 
This instance should be accessible only from within the VPC, allowing for secure communication and resource management within the AWS environment.

Create a VPC named datacenter-priv-vpc with the CIDR block 10.0.0.0/16.

Create a subnet named datacenter-priv-subnet inside the VPC with the CIDR block 10.0.1.0/24 and auto-assign IP option must not be enabled.

Create an EC2 instance named datacenter-priv-ec2 inside the subnet and instance type must be t2.micro.

Ensure the security group of the EC2 instance allows access only from within the VPC's CIDR block.

Create the main.tf file (do not create a separate .tf file) to provision the VPC, subnet and EC2 instance.

Use variables.tf file with the following variable names:

KKE_VPC_CIDR for the VPC CIDR block.
KKE_SUBNET_CIDR for the subnet CIDR block.
Use the outputs.tf file with the following variable names:

KKE_vpc_name for the name of the VPC.
KKE_subnet_name for the name of the subnet.
KKE_ec2_private for the name of the EC2 instance.

Notes:

The Terraform working directory is /home/bob/terraform.

Right-click under the EXPLORER section in VS Code and select Open in Integrated Terminal to launch the terminal.

Before submitting the task, ensure that terraform plan returns No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.

SOLUTION
Absolutely. For this KodeKloud task, keep the configuration simple: all VPC, subnet, security group, and EC2 resources go into main.tf, with variables in variables.tf and outputs in outputs.tf.

Since your Terraform environment has previously been using the AWS provider with LocalStack, keep your existing provider.tf and don't create another provider configuration.

1. Go to the Terraform directory
cd /home/bob/terraform

Check what is already there:

ls

You should have something like:

main.tf
provider.tf
variables.tf
outputs.tf

If main.tf, variables.tf, or outputs.tf don't exist, create them.

2. Create variables.tf
sudo vi variables.tf

Put:

variable "KKE_VPC_CIDR" {
  type    = string
  default = "10.0.0.0/16"
}

variable "KKE_SUBNET_CIDR" {
  type    = string
  default = "10.0.1.0/24"
}
Why?

The task specifically requires these two variable names:

KKE_VPC_CIDR → VPC CIDR
KKE_SUBNET_CIDR → subnet CIDR
3. Create main.tf
sudo vi main.tf

Use:

resource "aws_vpc" "datacenter_priv_vpc" {
  cidr_block = var.KKE_VPC_CIDR

  tags = {
    Name = "datacenter-priv-vpc"
  }
}

resource "aws_subnet" "datacenter_priv_subnet" {
  vpc_id                  = aws_vpc.datacenter_priv_vpc.id
  cidr_block              = var.KKE_SUBNET_CIDR
  map_public_ip_on_launch = false

  tags = {
    Name = "datacenter-priv-subnet"
  }
}

resource "aws_security_group" "datacenter_priv_sg" {
  name        = "datacenter-priv-sg"
  description = "Allow traffic only from within the VPC"
  vpc_id      = aws_vpc.datacenter_priv_vpc.id

  ingress {
    description = "Allow traffic from VPC"
    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = [var.KKE_VPC_CIDR]
  }

  egress {
    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }

  tags = {
    Name = "datacenter-priv-sg"
  }
}

resource "aws_instance" "datacenter_priv_ec2" {
  ami           = "ami-0c02fb55956c7d316"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
  subnet_id     = aws_subnet.datacenter_priv_subnet.id

  vpc_security_group_ids = [
    aws_security_group.datacenter_priv_sg.id
  ]

  tags = {
    Name = "datacenter-priv-ec2"
  }
}
Important parts

The subnet has:

map_public_ip_on_launch = false

This satisfies the requirement that auto-assignment of public IPs must not be enabled.

The EC2 is placed directly into the new subnet:

subnet_id = aws_subnet.datacenter_priv_subnet.id

And the security group only permits inbound traffic from:

cidr_blocks = [var.KKE_VPC_CIDR]

Since KKE_VPC_CIDR is 10.0.0.0/16, the instance accepts inbound traffic only from 10.0.0.0/16.

4. Create outputs.tf
sudo vi outputs.tf

Put:

output "KKE_vpc_name" {
  value = aws_vpc.datacenter_priv_vpc.tags["Name"]
}

output "KKE_subnet_name" {
  value = aws_subnet.datacenter_priv_subnet.tags["Name"]
}

output "KKE_ec2_private" {
  value = aws_instance.datacenter_priv_ec2.tags["Name"]
}

The required output variable names are therefore exactly:

KKE_vpc_name
KKE_subnet_name
KKE_ec2_private
5. Format the Terraform files

Run:

terraform fmt

This doesn't create infrastructure. It simply formats your .tf files into Terraform's standard style.

Then:

terraform validate

You want:

Success! The configuration is valid.
6. Initialize Terraform

If this directory hasn't already been initialized:

terraform init

If it is already initialized, you can still run it safely.

7. Run Terraform plan
terraform plan

The first time, you should see resources that Terraform intends to create.

You should have 4 resources:

aws_vpc
aws_subnet
aws_security_group
aws_instance

Then apply:

terraform apply

When asked:

Do you want to perform these actions?

enter:

yes
8. Verify the outputs

Run:

terraform output

You should see values corresponding to:

KKE_vpc_name      = "datacenter-priv-vpc"
KKE_subnet_name   = "datacenter-priv-subnet"
KKE_ec2_private   = "datacenter-priv-ec2"
9. Most important: final terraform plan

After terraform apply, run:

terraform plan

The final result should be:

No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.

That is exactly what the task's validator is asking for.

Final structure

Your directory should essentially contain:

/home/bob/terraform/
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
├── outputs.tf
└── provider.tf

Don't create separate files for the VPC, subnet, security group, or EC2. All four resources belong in main.tf as requested.

One thing to watch: if your existing provider.tf uses a specific LocalStack-compatible AMI or your lab's environment expects a different AMI ID, use that AMI rather than changing the provider configuration.
